<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Church of the Holy Spirit, Heidelberg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Heiliggeistkirche Heidelberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Church of the Holy Spirit is the main church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>'s old town, built in Gothic style from 1398 and the burial place of several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> palatine. Its galleries once housed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibliotheca-palatina">Bibliotheca Palatina</a>. From 1706 it was divided by a wall between Catholics and Protestants, and a dispute over its use in 1719–1720 led Elector Charles III Philip to move his residence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-spirit-heidelberg.jpg" alt="Church of the Holy Spirit, Heidelberg" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hlohning · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heidelberg_20060420_021.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of 1239 mentions a Romanesque chapel of the Holy Ghost in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, and an aisleless Gothic church, the Chapel of the Holy Spirit, stood on the site from around 1300. King Rupert commissioned the present building, the third church there, as the burial place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">Electors</a> of the Palatinate and the chief church of their seat. Its cornerstone was laid in 1398, the choir was consecrated in 1411 and the nave finished in 1441. Arnold Rype, a former mayor, is named as its master builder, a term then meaning the man who paid for it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The church changed confession more than ten times between the 16th and 20th centuries. The first Protestant service was held there in 1546; Elector Otto Henry made it a Lutheran parish church in 1557, and his successor Friedrich III turned it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> and commissioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a>. When Elector Karl III Philipp had soldiers tear down the dividing wall in 1719, pressure from other states and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> forced him to rebuild it. The wall was removed in 1886, put up again during the Kulturkampf, and finally taken down on 24 June 1936; the church has been solely Protestant since.</p><p>The galleries, chosen for their light, held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibliotheca-palatina">Bibliotheca Palatina</a>, which gathered the libraries of the university, the castle and several monasteries. In 1622, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years War</a>, the Count of Tilly took it as loot and presented it to the Pope; some 54 oxcarts of books reportedly went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>, 847 German manuscripts returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> in 1816. French troops raided the church in 1693, during the Palatine War of Succession, and a fire destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a>' tombs and the tower, which was rebuilt in baroque style in 1709.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Only the tomb of the founder, Rupert III, survives. A vault of the nave keeps a fresco of around 1440 showing eight angels with instruments, restored in 1950. The medieval glass was lost in 1693. Of 22 windows commissioned from Johannes Schreiter in 1977, only the Physics Window, installed in 1984 with Einstein's E=mc2 and the date of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> bombing, was made before protests ended the project in 1986; Hella Santarossa's five windows of broken coloured glass were later placed in the north nave. The church is now a parish church of the Evangelical Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Church of the Holy Spirit, Heidelberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
